': 'prepare_quiz:6078ea49-346c-afef-31d8-bcfbbe7b20fd', 'checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:6078ea49-346c-afef-31d8-bcfbbe7b20fd'}, 'parent_ids': ['21c76ee6-add3-40b4-bb55-bd7c4880f6a6', '6d7aaa95-c972-4266-8c36-d525f7b2d6df']}{'event': 'on_chain_stream', 'run_id': '67a575f4-8780-4d28-a882-7d65afd4c398', 'name': 'prepare_quiz', 'tags': ['seq:step:1'], 'metadata': {'flow': 'quiz', 'thread_id': '2d5e8d02-03a5-41fd-af1a-24335d850e99', 'langgraph_step': 2, 'langgraph_node': 'prepare_quiz', 'langgraph_triggers': ('branch:to:prepare_quiz',), 'langgraph_path': ('__pregel_pull', 'prepare_quiz'), 'langgraph_checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:6078ea49-346c-afef-31d8-bcfbbe7b20fd', 'checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:6078ea49-346c-afef-31d8-bcfbbe7b20fd'}, 'data': {'chunk': {'question': 'Explanation:  \nMultiplication is one of the four fundamental arithmetic operations. At its core, multiplying two numbers means adding one of them to itself as many times as specified by the other. For example, 4 × 3 means 4 added to itself 3 times (4 + 4 + 4 = 12).\n\nKey properties and methods:\n\n• Commutative property  \n  a × b = b × a  \n  You can swap the order of factors without changing the product.\n\n• Associative property  \n  (a × b) × c = a × (b × c)  \n  When multiplying more than two numbers, you can group them in any way.\n\n• Distributive property over addition  \n  a × (b + c) = a × b + a × c  \n  This allows you to break complex multiplications into simpler parts.\n\n• Long multiplication algorithm  \n  1. Multiply each digit of the bottom number by each digit of the top number, writing partial products.  \n  2. Shift each partial product one place to the left for each step down.  \n  3. Sum the partial products to get the final answer.\n\n• Special shortcuts  \n  – Multiplying near round numbers (using difference of squares)  \n    (a + b)(a – b) = a² – b²  \n  – Multiplying by powers of 10 (just append zeros)  \n\nUnderstanding these properties lets you tackle large or tricky multiplications more efficiently, often reducing the need for lengthy digit-by-digit work.\n\nQuestion:  \nUse a suitable property of multiplication (for example, the distributive property or a difference of squares identity) to compute 1,000,001 × 999,999 without performing the full long multiplication.'}}, 'parent_ids': ['21c76ee6-add3-40b4-bb55-bd7c4880f6a6', '6d7aaa95-c972-4266-8c36-d525f7b2d6df']}{'event': 'on_chain_end', 'data': {'output': {'question': 'Explanation:  \nMultiplication is one of the four fundamental arithmetic operations. At its core, multiplying two numbers means adding one of them to itself as many times as specified by the other. For example, 4 × 3 means 4 added to itself 3 times (4 + 4 + 4 = 12).\n\nKey properties and methods:\n\n• Commutative property  \n  a × b = b × a  \n  You can swap the order of factors without changing the product.\n\n• Associative property  \n  (a × b) × c = a × (b × c)  \n  When multiplying more than two numbers, you can group them in any way.\n\n• Distributive property over addition  \n  a × (b + c) = a × b + a × c  \n  This allows you to break complex multiplications into simpler parts.\n\n• Long multiplication algorithm  \n  1. Multiply each digit of the bottom number by each digit of the top number, writing partial products.  \n  2. Shift each partial product one place to the left for each step down.  \n  3. Sum the partial products to get the final answer.\n\n• Special shortcuts  \n  – Multiplying near round numbers (using difference of squares)  \n    (a + b)(a – b) = a² – b²  \n  – Multiplying by powers of 10 (just append zeros)  \n\nUnderstanding these properties lets you tackle large or tricky multiplications more efficiently, often reducing the need for lengthy digit-by-digit work.\n\nQuestion:  \nUse a suitable property of multiplication (for example, the distributive property or a difference of squares identity) to compute 1,000,001 × 999,999 without performing the full long multiplication.'}, 'input': {'topic': '